Here's a breakdown of each function, followed by a visual diagram of how they connect:
setup() runs once on boot. It initializes the LCD, attempts to mount the SD card, and calls scanSD() and displayMenu() to get the UI ready.
loop() is the main event loop. It routes to either handleMenuNavigation() or handleReadingMode() depending on whether a book is open.
scanSD() opens the SD root directory and iterates through files, collecting any .txt or .TXT files into the fileList[] array.
displayMenu() renders the book selection screen — it shows the currently selected filename (minus the .txt extension) centered on the LCD with scroll hints.
handleMenuNavigation() reads the joystick's Y-axis to scroll through books. A button press opens the selected book, loads its saved bookmark position from flash via Preferences, then fast-forwards to that page using skipPageSilent().
handleReadingMode() reads the joystick's X-axis to page forward or backward through the book using the pageHistory[] array. A short press does nothing; holding the button for 5 seconds saves the current position to flash and returns to the menu.
showPage() renders the current page to the LCD. It reads words one at a time from the file, wrapping them across the 4 rows × 20 columns display. When a word won't fit on the current row it moves to the next; if it won't fit at all, it seeks the file pointer back so that word becomes the start of the next page.
skipPageSilent() does exactly what showPage() does — the same word-wrap logic — but without writing anything to the LCD. It's used during bookmark restoration to advance the file pointer a full page at a time without flickering the screen.
getUniqueKey() generates a short, stable key (max 15 chars) for storing bookmarks in flash. Long filenames are shortened to first 7 + last 7 characters to fit within Preferences key length limits.
